{"Images": [{"Name": "i0", "Description": "Cholesterol crystal embolism - associated acute kidney injury (CCE-AKI)", "Path": "images/CCE-AKI-arteryv2", "Width": 617, "Height": 653, "MaxLevel": 0}], "Header": "Cholesterol crystal embolism\u2013associated acute kidney injury (CCE-AKI) is characterized by the obstruction of small renal vessels by cholesterol crystals released from atherosclerotic plaques. These emboli induce endothelial injury, activation of the coagulation system (\u201ccrystal clot\u201d), and immune cell infiltration, predominantly involving myeloid cells. Complement activation has been proposed as a key contributor to CCE-AKI pathophysiology, based primarily on in vitro and in vivo experimental models. Because vascular injury is central to CCE-AKI, the renal arterial compartment represents a key site for investigating these processes. Here, we demonstrate in situ complement activation within small renal arteries of patients with CCE-AKI.", "Rotation": 0, "Layout": {"Grid": [["i0"]]}, "Stories": [{"Name": "", "Description": "", "Waypoints": [{"Name": "Structure of the artery", "Description": "The renal artery plays a critical role in maintaining kidney perfusion and delivering oxygen and nutrients to renal tissues. Its vascular wall is composed of endothelial cells lining the lumen and smooth muscle cells that regulate blood flow. ", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Artery", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.4270312500000001, "Pan": [0.47243491577335395, 0.5809288246522157]}, {"Name": "Complement activation", "Description": "The complement is a part of the immune system. It can be activated by three different pathways : the classical, lectin or alternative pathway.\nHere, complement deposits including components of the classical pathway (C1q, C1r, C1s) and the lectin pathway (MBL) are observed on the endothelium, colocalizaing with C4 deposits (using anti-C4d antibody).", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Complement activation", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.44296875000000013, "Pan": [0.4724349157733537, 0.45621457158892625]}, {"Name": "Complement activation pt. 2", "Description": "Complement activation results in the formation of C3 convertases, consistent with the observed C3 deposits (detected using anti-C3c and anti-C3d antibodies), and can progress to terminal pathway activation and formation of the C5b-9 membrane attack complex, as suggested by C9 deposition.", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [], "Group": "Complement activation ", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.4270312500000001, "Pan": [0.4724349157733538, 0.6357331481668681]}, {"Name": "Macrophage infiltration", "Description": "C5aR1-expressing myeloid cells, predominantly macrophages, surround the small arteries, suggesting a spatial association between local complement activation and the presence of complement-responsive immune cells.", "Arrows": [], "Overlays": [{"x": 0.25167628667224384, "y": 0.533409351440127, "width": 0.28174167581412357, "height": 0.25003049152335644}], "Group": "Macrophages", "Masks": [], "ActiveMasks": [], "Zoom": 0.42703125000000014, "Pan": [0.4724349157733537, 0.4840130836025858]}]}], "Channels": [{"Rendered": false, "Name": "DAPI", "Path": "DAPI_0__DAPI"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C1q", "Path": "C1q_1__C1q"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C5aR1", "Path": "C5aR1_4__C5aR1"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "CD68", "Path": "CD68_5__CD68"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C3c", "Path": "C3c_6__C3c"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "CD163", "Path": "CD163_7__CD163"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C3d", "Path": "C3d_8__C3d"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "CD45", "Path": "CD45_9__CD45"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C1r", "Path": "C1r_10__C1r"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "CD20", "Path": "CD20_11__CD20"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C1s", "Path": "C1s_12__C1s"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "Ki67", "Path": "Ki67_13__Ki67"}, {"Rendered": false, "Name": "C4d", "Path": "C4d_14__C4d"}, {"Rendered": false, 